Emacs has got somewhat bigger (and hence was smaller, perhaps, when sj was
using them); different unixes produce quite different executable sizes for
some reason, perhaps not that closely correlated with different resident
set sizes; graphical Web browsers have got colossally bigger. But if sj
wasn't using both at the very same time, he could probably endure each one
paging in when he came to use it.

I expect, even these days, a machine with 128MB will be fine for anything
except big-ticket X environments (stick to a lean window manager without a
bunch of integrated crap) and graphical Web browsers (learn to love w3m).
